    sections = [
        ("What's on the lots", table(["Finding", "Cause", "Approach"], [["Driveway settled at the garage", "Uncompacted 1960s backfill", "Replace; compact the strip; dowel to apron"], ["Orange stains on the driveway", "Iron in well-water irrigation", "Redirect heads, oxalic cleaner, sealer"], ["Patio slab cracked in the middle", "No joints, 20-ft panel", "Replace with joints at 8 ft"], ["Shed pad sinking", "Precast blocks on sand", "Poured 4-in. pad on compacted base"], ["Drain field in the way", "Septic layout", "Site the slab or pavers clear of it; the county checks"]], "South Venice patterns")),
        ("Ground and water", p("Myakka fine sand with a shallow wet-season water table, Alligator Creek and Lemon Bay setting the AE zones, and septic systems that dictate where anything can be built. Concrete here wants a compacted base and a slope to a swale; pavers want the same base plus an edge that holds.")),
        ("The septic system decides where the slab goes", p("This is the constraint that catches people out here and in few other parts of the service area. A large share of South Venice lots run on a private well and an on-site septic system, and Florida's on-site sewage rules in Administrative Code Chapter 64E-6 keep both clear of new construction: a drain field has to stay a set distance from a potable well, from the property line, and from any structure placed over it. A slab poured across a drain field is less a permit problem than a plumbing problem you have bought, because the field needs air, water has to reach it, and a truck has to be able to pump the tank.") +
                                                            p("So the first thing worth finding on a South Venice job is the septic permit on file with the Florida Department of Health in Sarasota County, which shows where the tank and the field actually sit. Where that record is missing, and on 1950s and 1960s lots it often is, the layout gets probed before anything is formed. A patio that has to shift six feet to clear a field is a ten-minute conversation at the estimate and a demolition bill after the pour.")),
        ("A worked example", p("A 1971 Venice Gardens home with a 420-square-foot driveway in five pieces and a rust stripe from the sprinkler. Replacement on four compacted inches of crushed base, 4-inch 4,000 PSI fiber concrete with control joints every 8 feet, sprinkler heads moved, and a penetrating sealer at 28 days. County right-of-way use permit for the apron. Planning band in September 2026: $5,000 to $7,500.")),
    ]
    return city_page("south-venice", "Concrete & Pavers in South Venice & Venice Gardens, FL", "Driveway replacement, repair, patio slabs and pads in South Venice and Venice Gardens: county permits through the Venice office, 1950s to 80s block homes, septic and well constraints, and a 2026 worked example.",
                     "Concrete and pavers in South Venice and Venice Gardens", "Unincorporated county, small platted lots from the 1950s to 80s, septic and well water, and original driveways that have done their time.", intro, sections, faq)


